It also obeys the ordinary product rule in either entry. Expanding the derivatives of $AB$ gives $\begin{aligned}
 \{AB,C\}
 &=\frac{\partial(AB)}{\partial q_i}
   \frac{\partial C}{\partial p_i}\notag\\
 &\quad-\frac{\partial(AB)}{\partial p_i}
   \frac{\partial C}{\partial q_i}\notag\\
 &=A\left(
   \frac{\partial B}{\partial q_i}\frac{\partial C}{\partial p_i}
  -\frac{\partial B}{\partial p_i}\frac{\partial C}{\partial q_i}
  \right)\notag\\
 &\quad+B\left(
   \frac{\partial A}{\partial q_i}\frac{\partial C}{\partial p_i}
  -\frac{\partial A}{\partial p_i}\frac{\partial C}{\partial q_i}
  \right)\notag\\
 &=A\{B,C\}+B\{A,C\}.
 \end{aligned}$ Repeated indices are summed in this calculation.

